-var VERSION="3.34.0-agent-discovery-2"; /* 3.31.4 ASCII-SOURCE-1 (2026-10-25, completing SEO-INDEX-9): the one remaining U+2014 in the versions JSON Feed title is now the \u2014 escape, so the file is pure ASCII for the Latin-1 canonical deploy and the feed still shows the dash. The work-with-me suite's pure-ASCII assertion is green. */ /* 3.31.3 SEO-INDEX-9 (2026-10-25): pure-ASCII guard repair. The 3.31.2 pass removed two of the three U+2014 characters commit 941c245 put in this file and left one, so the repository guard stayed red on main. Every remaining non-ASCII character is an ASCII hyphen; no behaviour changes, only the glyph in a string. */  /* 3.31.2 SEO-INDEX-8 (2026-10-25, guard repair): main was red for two reasons found after 3.31.1 shipped. (a) commit 941c245 (a concurrent session) put U+2014 em-dashes in two RSS titles, so the repository guard "worker.js is pure ASCII" failed on main; the titles now use an ASCII hyphen and the guard passes. (b) my own seo-index assertion still matched the pre-941c245 sitemap SQL string ("updated_at, version FROM papers"); 941c245 replaced it with COALESCE(last_revised_at, updated_at, created_at), which is strictly better freshness signal, so the assertion was stale rather than the code being wrong - it now accepts the newer form instead of pinning one literal. Both are guard repairs: the behaviour 3.31.1 shipped is unchanged. */  /* 3.31.1 SEO-INDEX-7 (2026-10-25, found by the post-deploy continuation probe): TELEMETRY-COVERAGE-1 - seo_crawl_log was created at 09:33 and the cron published seo_crawl_googlebot_7d = 0 within minutes, so a 7-day count was computed from a window that predates the data source and the trigger fired a false crawl-outage. A count is now published only when the log covers 2 or more distinct days; before that the key is written empty, which the trigger view reads as no value and therefore no hit, and seo_crawl_coverage_days records how much history actually exists. This is the same defect class the red team found in 3.30.2: a number that cannot be true is worse than no number. */  /* 3.31.0 LISTING-VERSION-1 (2026-10-25): new papers, revised papers and version depth are first-class. papers.first_published_at / last_revised_at / version_semver / version_sort / version_count drive the index (sort=new|revised|version), the paper header (Published + Revised + version count), sitemap lastmod, llms.txt, OAI/JSON API records, and two feeds: /rss.xml for new papers and /rss/versions.xml + /feed-versions.json for revisions. lastBuildDate is the newest item date, not the request time. */ /* 3.30.5 SEO-INDEX-6 (2026-10-25, red-team follow-up): REDTEAM-DOUBLE-COUNT-1 - after 3.30.4 removed the /about hub the audit endpoint still added its own literal 6, so GET /api/seo/audit reported sitemapPaperUrls 704 against a 703-URL sitemap. The count is one named constant (SEO_PAPER_HUB_COUNT) read by the metric publisher and by the audit, so the report and the artifact cannot disagree again. */  /* 3.30.4 SEO-INDEX-5 (2026-10-25, CMD RED TEAM FAIL on 3.30.2; renumbered because a concurrent session deployed 3.30.3-ga4-collect-restore while this fix was queued). Two measured defects in my own artifact. (a) REDTEAM-SITEMAP-404-1: the papers sitemap listed /about, a qnfo.org route that answers 404 on papers.qnfo.org, so the fix shipped the exact soft-404 crawl waste it was auditing for; the hub is gone and the covered count is one named constant. (b) TRUNCATED-DETAIL-1: indexNowLog stored detail.slice(0,1000) and a 7-chunk weekly submission serialises to ~2.8 KB, so the JSON was cut mid-object, the engine counter threw, and seo_indexnow_engines_ok published 0 - a false engine outage every Monday on the one metric that certifies multi-engine reach. Errors are shortened, the budget is 4000, and the reader counts accepted engines from the raw text when a parse fails. */  /* 3.30.3 GA4-COLLECT-RESTORE-1 (2026-10-25, owner report: Google Analytics is not collecting recent data for qnfo.org): measured from the GA4 Data API, papers.qnfo.org served 84-203 page views a day through 2026-10-12, then the property received 0 rows on 2026-10-13 and 1-8 a day after; no qnfo.org or papers.qnfo.org row exists after 10-12. The Zaraz GA4 action does fire at the edge (debug: 'Action called: Pageview from Google Analytics 4') but issues no request and GA4 stores nothing. ZARAZ-GA4-1 (5e88c1e0, 2026-10-12 14:00) removed this gtag.js on the assumption Zaraz counted every page view once; it counted zero. The direct tag is restored and the Zaraz loader is removed, so gtag.js sends one page view and there is no second counter. */ /* 3.30.2 SEO-INDEX-3 (2026-10-25): a metric written once at launch goes stale, which is the registry-model defect in another register. The hourly cron now republishes the four crawl measurements into qnfo-audit.analytics_dash_meta, which is what v_metric_trigger_state reads: seo_crawl_googlebot_7d, seo_crawl_bingbot_7d, seo_indexnow_engines_ok and seo_sitemap_urls. Each is computed from the row-level source (living-paper.seo_crawl_log, indexnow_log.detail, papers) at publish time, so the trigger cannot report a number that no longer matches its evidence, and the values age by at most one hour instead of forever. */  /* 3.30.1 SEO-INDEX-2 (2026-10-25, first live run of 3.30.0): the fan-out proved the engine gap and exposed a second one. Live detail from papers.qnfo.org: yandex 200, seznam 200, naver 200, api.indexnow.org THREW, www.bing.com THREW - both from the Cloudflare egress, while the same POST from an ordinary address answers 200 for both. So the shared relay and Bing are not merely rate-limited from Workers, they are unreachable, and a Worker cannot be the only submitter for the index behind ChatGPT search and Copilot. This version (a) records the real error text per endpoint instead of a mangled status, so the next run names the failure; (b) adds GET /api/indexnow/urls, the public, side-effect-free list the GitHub Actions fallback submits from (their egress is not the Workers one) - SLA-AND-PATHS-1: two independent paths to the same DoD. */  /* 3.30.0 SEO-INDEX-1 (2026-10-25, owner report: Google Search Console is not crawling QNFO papers; papers do not show in search results or AI prompts). Discovery is now measured at our own edge and pushed on EVERY engine: IndexNow fans out to all five endpoints instead of stopping at yandex (Bing, the index behind ChatGPT search and Copilot, had never received a URL); crawler requests are logged to living-paper.seo_crawl_log and summarised by /api/seo/crawl; /api/seo/audit is the metric source of truth; the sitemap advertises updated_at (a revised paper used to look unchanged); /papers/<slug>/ 308s to the canonical slug; /papers/<slug>.md serves the paper as Markdown for agents; robots.txt names the AI crawlers; llms.txt lists the whole corpus under one heading. Deliberate non-change: lpDoi() still drops every identifier of the retired deposit prefix, because all 575 recorded ones are 410 tombstones (the depositing account was blocked 2026-10-07, reason out-of-scope) and publishing a dead identifier is worse than publishing none. */  /* 3.29.7 PLAIN-FILL-MEMORY-1 (2026-10-25, remediation sweep of the dashboard's 59 metric breaches; pillar reliability): the hourly cron had not completed since 2026-10-20 06:03 (fleet_heartbeat stuck at 3.29.0) and was killed with exceededMemory 21-24 times a day, starting the afternoon PLAIN-FILL-CADENCE-1 put plainFill on that cron. plainFill read p.body_md for every published paper (LIMIT 2000, 693 papers at >= 20,000 chars) into one isolate; it now takes 120 candidates ordered by need (no plain_summary row first) and still builds PLAIN_FILL_MAX=12 a run, so IndexNow, rate-limit pruning, quick-read pruning, render-health, the browser math sample and paper filing after it run again. */ /* 3.29.0 READER-REPORT-1 (2026-10-19, pillar research): a reader can report a typo, awkward wording, a factual error or a citation problem at the passage it is in (select text, then Report; or the line at the end of each paper). POST /api/paper-report stores it anonymously in qnfo-audit.reader_reports behind a hidden field, a minimum time on the page and per-address and daily caps; one model checks it against the paper, and a confirmed report becomes an internal_errata row the revision loop folds into the next version. The hourly tick retries pending checks. */ /* 3.28.1 ABOUT-LABEL-TRUNC-1 (2026-10-19, pillar reach, agent_issues 100158): the "How to check the work" links on /about carry each paper's full title; the last one was the slug cut at its length limit ("... via distinct lo"). */ /* 3.28.0 CLAIM-INTEGRITY-1 (2026-10-19, pillar research): /papers/<slug>/ serves a paper whose status is retracted (qnfo-research-exec 0.22.0 retracts a paper that described experiments or reviews as done that never took place), so its link shows the retraction notice instead of a 404; listings, feeds, the sitemap and the PDF route still read the published statuses only. */ /* 3.27.0 CRON-GRID-1 (owner directive 2026-10-19, pillar autonomy): the trigger is hourly; the daily 06:00 run (IndexNow, prunes, plain fill, render health, shelving) runs on the 06:00 fire and is retried hourly, at most 3 times, while its last good run is over 24.5 h old. Every fire is logged to cron_fire_log for CRON-WATCH-1. */ /* 3.26.5 GW-PAPER-AUTHORS-SCALAR-1 (2026-10-19, agent_issues 100061, pillar reach): paperAuthors fell back to splitting the raw text when the authors column held JSON that is neither a list nor a string, so "null", "123", "true" and an object became author names in the byline, the JSON-LD, the feeds and /api/papers. gwAuthorList now reads such text as no author (an object with a string name field is its one author) and splits only text that is not JSON; "Surname, Given" stays one author only when the given names are more than one word or the surname is Quni-Gudzinas, so "Alice, Bob" is two authors. Live rows hold none of these shapes today (744 arrays, 250 plain names, 1 comma list), so the change guards the next import. paper-fields.test.mjs carries the cases. paper-fields.test.mjs also asserts the gwSplitKeywords line that 3.26.4 (#2679, CF-OBS-ISSUE-1) added, which the squash of PR 2680 deleted with a VERSION rollback to 3.26.3 and 4f20a3e restored on main: a JSON string holding keywords reads as that one keyword. */ /* 3.26.1 (2026-10-19, RESOLVE-ALL-OPEN-1): carries the 3.25.x fixes of PR 2603 (GW-JSON-QUIET, PAPER-FIELDS-1, paper-context stack, FILL_MODEL floor) on top of 3.26.0 PLAIN-FILL-1; PLAIN-NEGATIVE-CACHE-1 is dropped because PLAIN-FILL-1 moved the build off the request path. */ /* 3.26.0 PLAIN-FILL-1 (2026-10-19): the digest (GET /papers/<slug>/quickread and /api/quickread/<slug> without q) read the plain-language summary through plainSummary, which built it with up to two model calls on a cache miss, so a crawler following a link spent money (QUICK-READ-DIGEST-1: nothing to type, no spend) and quick-read.test.mjs failed on main. Every GET now reads plain_summary only (plainGet); the daily cron builds missing or stale summaries, PLAIN_FILL_MAX per run, newest papers first (plainFill). */ /* 3.24.3 QUICK-READ-DIGEST-4 (2026-10-19, the third pass over live reads): (a) /api/quickread/<slug> answered bad_slug for the library's dotted slugs (x3.3-bosonic-codes-native-encoding) because the guard allowed only letters, digits and hyphens; it now allows the dot and still refuses "..". (b) the reading map ended with a "References" row; a bibliography is not a place to read the argument, so it is left out. */ /* 3.24.2 QUICK-READ-DIGEST-3 (2026-10-19, the rest of the first live map): the map listed the right sections but with "0 words" against every one whose prose lives in its h3 subsections (joules-per-solution-metric: 8 of 11 rows read 0), which is a row a reader cannot use. A section's length is now its own text plus every deeper section that follows it up to the next heading at its depth or above, and a heading with nothing under it at all is left out of the map. */ /* 3.24.1 QUICK-READ-DIGEST-2 (2026-10-19, first live reads of 3.24.0): the reading map listed ONE heading on joules-per-solution-metric because that paper carries its title as its own "#" heading, so every real section is an h3 after the renderer shifts the outline down a level and an h2-only map saw the title and nothing else. qdMap now skips the heading whose name is the paper's title and takes the first depth that yields a map (h2 when there are three or more, else h3, else whatever exists); quick-read.test.mjs carries that corpus shape (chaptered-work). */ /* 3.24.0 QUICK-READ-DIGEST-1 (2026-10-19, owner directive: "there is no difference between Quick Read and Ask this paper"): it was true  -  QUICK-READ-1..6 read the paper for a QUESTION, which is what the paper page's Ask rail already does, so the reader met two doors to one room. /papers/<slug>/quickread and /api/quickread/<slug> with no question now return a DIGEST: the abstract verbatim, the claim, the figures the paper reports, how it is done, what would falsify it, where the claim stops, and a map of where to read (each section with its length), every line a sentence of the paper with the section id the paper page publishes, no model call, no cache, no rate limit. The question view is unchanged at ?q= (old links, the API, the audit). The index row's Quick read no longer carries the query, and the paper page's Quick read button says what it now does. */ /* 3.23.2 QUICK-READ-6 (2026-10-19, found in the closeout sweep): a live payload said coverage "related" with passages 0 and chars 0 - the semantic pick had filtered every candidate out (the closest sections were shorter than the 80-character floor) and the page still claimed it had read them. qrPick now takes the mode (a semantic ranking gates on cosine, not on lexical hits), and the semantic branch falls back to the honest "none" verdict when nothing usable was picked rather than labelling an empty page "related". /* 3.23.1 QUICK-READ-5 (2026-10-19, claim audit): QUICK-READ-4 shipped /api/quickread and said in its PR body that openapi.json carried the endpoint; it did not. The path is now in the open index, and quick-read.test.mjs reads /openapi.json and fails if the endpoint disappears from it. /* 3.23.0 QUICK-READ-4 (2026-10-19, the two gaps the first live reads exposed): (a) the cache key was (paper, question) and a corrected paper kept serving its old quick read for up to 45 days, so the key now carries the paper's revision (pipeline version, version, updated_at, body length); (b) the ranking was exact-vocabulary, so a question phrased in other words was answered "this paper does not answer this question" - true of the words, false of the meaning - and the question and the sections are now embedded (bge-small, one batched call, vectors cached per revision in living-paper.quick_read_vec) before that verdict, with the closest sections labelled "related" on the page. Also: GET /_audit/quick-read reports which of the three paths answered and fails its invariant on an unreadable payload; /reading, llms.txt, openapi and /health carry the feature. QUICK-READ-3 (3.22.2): the quick read's first live reads took 12-16 s. On the same 2k-token prompt kimi-k2.6 answered the JSON in 9.7 s and gpt-oss-120b in 5.2 s with reasoning_effort "low" (14.0 s with the default; Harmony rejects "none" outright), so the quick read now asks gpt-oss-120b at effort low, the token ceiling stays 900 and the 15 s deadline stays with the extractive read behind it. QUICK-READ-2 (3.22.1), measured on the live corpus): QUICK-READ-1 shipped 3.21.0 and the first live reads took 12-16 s, one of them crossing the 20 s deadline, because the model was asked for up to ten items whose evidence could run to three sentences each. The model now returns ONE sentence of evidence per item, at most 3 answers and 2 of the other two, at 900 output tokens, and the deadline is 15 s with the deterministic extractive read behind it: the same page in roughly half the generation time, with the same guarantee that every line quotes the paper. /* 3.22.0 FILING-2 (2026-10-18, owner directive "fix all automatically", pillar autonomy): the daily pass now files BOTH layers. A newly published paper gets its shelf and its tags in the same run, so the tag vocabulary cannot thin out as the corpus grows; the tag prompt is built from tag_registry, so adding or retiring a tag is one D1 row and no deploy. The same pass sweeps orphan paper_tag rows. The index renders the whole vocabulary (up to 64 chips) instead of the top 14. */ /* 3.21.0-quick-read-1 /* 3.21.0 QUICK-READ-1 (2026-10-19, owner directive, pillar reach): Google Scholar's Quick Read (2026-08-25) shows one paper as it answers ONE question: overview, then Answers (each point with its supporting detail behind Show details), Approach and Considerations. This is that idea over the living-paper corpus, plus the one thing a corpus of full texts can do that a search index cannot  -  every line of the answer quotes a sentence of the paper, and a statement whose evidence is not found in the text is dropped and counted rather than shown. /papers/<slug>/quickread is the page (question form; POST renders server-side, no JavaScript needed), /api/quickread/<slug>?q= is the JSON, and living-paper.quick_read caches each (paper, question) pair for 45 days. Three paths to an answer: the cache under 300ms, one model call over the passages the question touches, and a deterministic extractive read of the paper's own sentences when the model fails or returns nothing quotable  -  so the page always answers and names which path ran. A question the paper never uses the words of is answered before any model call: coverage none, no spend. */ /* 3.18.12 ULA-V21-FOOTERS-1 (2026-10-18): QNFO-ULA v2.1 is live; the QWAV footer, papers footer, archive lede/description and ulaLatest fallback license labels changed v2.0 -> v2.1. */ /* 3.19.0 PAPER-AUDIENCE-CATEGORIES-1 (2026-10-18, owner directive, pillar reach): the papers library filed every paper into five keyword buckets and "Other" held 161 of 606 public papers (26%), the largest shelf on the site. Nine audience shelves replace them (energy per answer, error correction, physics, numbers & p-adics, logic & formalism, AI & knowledge, autonomous systems, assessments, cross-cutting essays), each named for the reader who looks for it; every paper's shelf is curated data in living-paper D1 (table paper_category), read first by the index, the archive, the paper page and the related panel, with the keyword classifier kept only as the fallback for a paper published before the daily filing job has seen it. ?category= other, number-theory and computer-science are aliased, so old links still land. */ /* 3.18.10 IDENTITY-WELLKNOWN-1 (2026-10-18): /.well-known/security.txt (RFC 9116) on every gateway host, /.well-known/atproto-did on qnfo.org for the Bluesky domain handle, rel="me" to the Mastodon profile in every page head. */ /* 3.18.5 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-qnfo-gateway-1 (2026-10-14, agent_issues 2533, child of 2342, pillar autonomy): the 6 server-side swallowed catches log (2 browser-script hits marked standards-ok or allowlisted) as a structured console.error (worker, where, error); control flow unchanged. */ /* 3.18.4 FLEET-HARDENING-2 (2026-10-13, agent_issues 2397, pillar autonomy): the daily cron ends with a qnfo-audit fleet_heartbeat upsert through a new AUDIT binding (ok=0 when the IndexNow step threw, which was swallowed before and is now logged). */ /* 3.18.3 HUB-CACHE-VERSION-1 (2026-10-13, pillar reach, failure mode zaraz-autoinject-worker-html): the qnfo.org hub copy in each colo's cache now carries the VERSION that built it, and a copy built by another version is rebuilt while the request waits up to 2.5 s instead of being served stale; the runner probe of 07:39 UTC read a pre-3.16.1 copy without /cdn-cgi/zaraz/i.js while every fresh render carried it. */ /* 3.18.2 PAGE-POLICY-1 (2026-10-13, pillar reach, agent_issues 2343 WEB-PERF-EDGE-1): every HTML response carries Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin (no-referrer when the query carries a token) and Permissions-Policy camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=() from the worker (qnfo.org had neither; the subdomains had them only from a zone rule), and an HTML response without Cache-Control gets public, max-age=300, s-maxage=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400 when it is a public GET 200, no-store otherwise; handlers that set their own are unchanged, JSON untouched. 3.18.1 RENDER-DEFECTS-2300 (2026-10-13, pillar reach, agent_issues 2300 2158 2357): PANDOC-FENCE-GLUE-1 reads a code fence glued to prose or to its last code line as a fence (ultrametric-intelligence printed its Python as prose and typeset its identifiers); MATH-RESIDUE-5 typesets a formula glued to a name by a product sign; RAIL-MATH-1 gives /api/paper-context title_tex and excerpt_tex (formulas kept as TeX, plain-text math typeset, currency escaped) and the Related and Versions panels paint them, since every residue the browser sample counted on chapter-1 and log20240505010101strangeloopbook on 2026-10-12 was panel text; a formula ending in an escaped brace is lifted as math; MathJax knows the Sha macro. 3.18.0 ACTIVITYPUB-KEY-1 (2026-10-12, pillar reach, #2249 #2413): the actor publishes publicKeyPem when AP_PUBLIC_KEY is set (cf-ops gen-activitypub-key); merged with main 3.17.1 (REACH-RENDER-1). */ /* 3.20.0 PAPER-TAGS-1 (2026-10-18, owner directive, pillar reach): a shelf is exclusive and answers "where does this live"; it cannot show that a QEC paper is also a Bruhat-Tits paper, or that a number-theory result helps choose a hardware platform. Tags are the second, multi-label layer over the nine shelves: method (what the paper works with), topic (what it is about), use (what a reader could do with it). 1,400 curated assignments over 606 papers, 41 controlled tags, in living-paper D1 (paper_tag + tag_registry), so a wrong tag is a DELETE. The index gains a tag row that composes with the shelf filter and search (?tag= + ?category=), paper cards show up to two tags, the paper page shows its full tag set, and /papers/tags is the catalogue grouped by kind with counts and definitions. Tag and shelf reads are per-isolate cached and degrade to shelves alone when the tables are absent. */ /* 3.18.12 ULA-V21-FOOTERS-1 (2026-10-18): QNFO-ULA v2.1 is live; the QWAV footer, papers footer, archive lede/description and ulaLatest fallback license labels changed v2.0 -> v2.1. */ /* 3.19.0 PAPER-AUDIENCE-CATEGORIES-1 (2026-10-18, owner directive, pillar reach): the papers library filed every paper into five keyword buckets and "Other" held 161 of 606 public papers (26%), the largest shelf on the site. Nine audience shelves replace them (energy per answer, error correction, physics, numbers & p-adics, logic & formalism, AI & knowledge, autonomous systems, assessments, cross-cutting essays), each named for the reader who looks for it; every paper's shelf is curated data in living-paper D1 (table paper_category), read first by the index, the archive, the paper page and the related panel, with the keyword classifier kept only as the fallback for a paper published before the daily filing job has seen it. ?category= other, number-theory and computer-science are aliased, so old links still land. */ /* 3.18.10 IDENTITY-WELLKNOWN-1 (2026-10-18): /.well-known/security.txt (RFC 9116) on every gateway host, /.well-known/atproto-did on qnfo.org for the Bluesky domain handle, rel="me" to the Mastodon profile in every page head. */ /* 3.18.5 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-qnfo-gateway-1 (2026-10-14, agent_issues 2533, child of 2342, pillar autonomy): the 6 server-side swallowed catches log (2 browser-script hits marked standards-ok or allowlisted) as a structured console.error (worker, where, error); control flow unchanged. */ /* 3.18.4 FLEET-HARDENING-2 (2026-10-1